"1. Introduction — PTX ISA 9.4 documentation (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/)\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/#parallel-synchronization-and-communication-instructions-redux-sync\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 31060\nL0: [Input: Search docs] [Input] [Input]\nL1: \nL2: 【0†PTX ISA】\nL3: \nL4: Parallel Thread Execution ISA Version 9.4\nL5: \nL6: The programming guide to using PTX (Parallel Thread Execution) and ISA (Instruction Set Architecture).\nL7: \nL8: # 1. 【1†Introduction】【1†】\nL9: \nL10: This document describes PTX, a low-level parallel thread execution virtual machine and instruction set architecture (ISA). PTX exposes the GPU as a data-parallel computing device.\nL11: ## 1.1. 【2†Scalable Data-Parallel Computing using GPUs】【2†】\nL12: Driven by the insatiable market demand for real-time, high-definition 3D graphics, the programmable GPU has evolved into a highly parallel, multithreaded, many-core processor with tremendous computational horsepower and very high memory bandwidth. The GPU is especially well-suited to address problems that can be expressed as data-parallel computations - the same program is executed on many data elements in parallel - with high arithmetic intensity - the ratio of arithmetic operations to memory operations.\nL13: Because the same program is executed for each data element, there is a lower requirement for sophisticated flow control; and because it is executed on many data elements and has high arithmetic intensity, the memory access latency can be hidden with calculations instead of big data caches.\nL14: Data-parallel processing maps data elements to parallel processing threads. Many applications that process large data sets can use a data-parallel programming model to speed up the computations. In 3D rendering large sets of pixels and vertices are mapped to parallel threads.\nL15: Similarly, image and media processing applications such as post-processing of rendered images, video encoding and decoding, image scaling, stereo vision, and pattern recognition can map image blocks and pixels to parallel processing threads. In fact, many algorithms outside the field of image rendering and processing are accelerated by data-parallel processing, from general signal processing or physics simulation to computational finance or computational biology.\nL16: PTX defines a virtual machine and ISA for general purpose parallel thread execution. PTX programs are translated at install time to the target hardware instruction set. The PTX-to-GPU translator and driver enable NVIDIA GPUs to be used as programmable parallel computers.\nL17: ## 1.2. 【3†Goals of PTX】【3†】\nL18: \nL19: PTX provides a stable programming model and instruction set for general purpose parallel programming. It is designed to be efficient on NVIDIA GPUs supporting the computation features defined by the NVIDIA Tesla architecture. High level language compilers for languages such as CUDA and C/C++ generate PTX instructions, which are optimized for and translated to native target-architecture instructions.\nL20: \nL21: The goals for PTX include the following:\nL22:   * Provide a stable ISA that spans multiple GPU generations.\nL23: \nL24:   * Achieve performance in compiled applications comparable to native GPU performance.\nL25: \nL26:   * Provide a machine-independent ISA for C/C++ and other compilers to target.\nL27: \nL28:   * Provide a code distribution ISA for application and middleware developers.\nL29: \nL30:   * Provide a common source-level ISA for optimizing code generators and translators, which map PTX to specific target machines.\nL31:   * Facilitate hand-coding of libraries, performance kernels, and architecture tests.\nL32: \nL33:   * Provide a scalable programming model that spans GPU sizes from a single unit to many parallel units.\nL34: ## 1.3. 【4†PTX ISA Version 9.4】【4†】\nL35: \nL36: PTX ISA version 9.4 introduces the following new features:\nL37: \nL38:   * Adds support for `sm_107` target architecture.\nL39: \nL40:   * Adds support for target `sm_107f` that supports family-specific features.\nL41: \nL42:   * Adds support for target `sm_107a` that supports architecture-specific features.\nL43: \nL44:   * Adds support for instruction types `.u8x4`, `.s8x4`, `.u16x2`, `.s16x2` for `set` instruction.\nL45: \nL46:   * Adds support for `.pzo` qualifier for `cvt` instruction.\nL47:   * Adds support for `.rz` rounding mode for `cvt` instruction with `.e4m3x2`, `.e5m2x2`, `.e2m3x2`, `.e3m2x2`, `.e2m1x2` destination types.\nL48: \nL49:   * Adds support for `.multicast::cluster::32b` qualifier for `mbarrier.expect_tx`, `mbarrier.complete_tx`, `mbarrier.arrive`, `mbarrier.arrive_drop`, `cp.async.bulk`, `cp.async.bulk.tensor` and `tcgen05.commit` instructions.\nL50: \nL51:   * Adds support for `.sync_restrict::shared::read::mma::a` qualifier for `tcgen05.commit` instruction.\nL52:   * Adds support for `.level::eviction_priority` qualifier for `cp.async.bulk.prefetch` and `cp.async.bulk.prefetch.tensor` instructions.\nL53: \nL54:   * Adds support for `applypriority.async.bulk` and `applypriority.async.bulk.tensor` instructions.\nL55: \nL56:   * Adds support for `.override::global_address` and `.override_attribute` qualifiers for `cp.async.bulk.tensor`, `cp.reduce.async.bulk.tensor`, `cp.async.bulk.prefetch.tensor` instructions.\nL57:   * Adds support for `.im2col_no_offs::w` qualifier for `cp.async.bulk.tensor` and `cp.reduce.async.bulk.tensor` instructions.\nL58: \nL59:   * Adds support for `.spcompress` qualifier for `tcgen05.ld` and `tcgen05.ld.red` instructions.\nL60: \nL61:   * Adds support for `spcompress` and `spdecompress` instructions.\nL62: \nL63:   * Adds support for `.ue5m3x2` instruction type for `cvt` instruction.\nL64: \nL65:   * Adds support for `.scaled::n1::ue8m0` qualifier for `cvt` instruction.\nL66:   * Adds `UE5M3` as scale type for `tcgen05.mma` and `tcgen05.mma.sp` instructions.\nL67: \nL68:   * Adds support for `.kind::ti16` qualifier for `tcgen05.mma`, `tcgen05.mma.sp`, `tcgen05.mma.ws`, `tcgen05.mma.ws.sp` instructions.\nL69: \nL70:   * Adds support for `add`, `sub`, `mul` and `fma` instructions with `.f16x2`/`.bf16x2`/`.f32x2` types.\nL71: \nL72:   * Adds support for `add`, `sub`, `mul` and `fma` instructions with FP8/FP6/FP4 x4 packed types.\nL73:   * Adds support for `.exclusive` qualifier for `tcgen05.alloc` and `tcgen05.dealloc` instructions.\nL74: \nL75:   * Adds support for `.phase_type::*` qualifier for `mbarrier.test_wait` and `mbarrier.try_wait` instructions.\nL76: \nL77:   * Adds support for `reportPredicate` and `reportValue` operands for `mbarrier.test_wait` and `mbarrier.try_wait` instructions.\nL78: \nL79:   * Adds support for `.layout` qualifier for mbarrier instructions and support for `mbarrier.check_layout` instruction.\nL80:   * Adds support for `.report_mechanism` qualifier for `cp.async.bulk` and `cp.async.bulk.tensor` instructions.\nL81: \nL82:   * Adds support for `.decompress::lut::b` qualifier for `tcgen05.mma` instruction.\nL83: \nL84:   * Adds support for `.collector::b::*` qualifier for `tcgen05.mma` instruction.\nL85: \nL86:   * Adds support for `.loc_intermediate` directive and `.nv_intermediate_source_section` construct for intermediate IR source location mapping.\nL87:   * Adds support for `.valid_addr` and `.L1::32B` qualifiers for `prefetch` instruction.\nL88: \nL89:   * Adds support for `.noftz` qualifier with `.f32` type for `atom`, `red`, `cp.reduce.async.bulk`, and `multimem.cp.reduce.async.bulk` instructions.\nL90: \nL91:   * Adds support for `.proxy::readonly` qualifier for `ld` instruction.\nL92: \nL93:   * Adds support for `.minperctamemory` directive and `%perctamemoryoffset`, `%perctamemorysize` special registers for per-CTA global memory.\nL94:   * Adds support for `.s8.s4` types for `ldmatrix` instruction for `.m8n16` shape.\nL95: \nL96:   * Adds support for `.sync_restrict::shared::cluster::read` qualifier for `fence.proxy.async::generic` instruction.\nL97: \nL98:   * Adds support for `.acquire` and `.release` qualifiers with `.alias` qualifier for `fence.proxy` instruction.\nL99: \nL100:   * Adds support for `fabric.try_get.async.tensor`, `fabric.try_put.async.tensor`, and `fabric.try_red.async.tensor` instructions.\nL101: \nL102:   * Adds support for `fabric.try_atom` instruction.\nL103:   * Adds support for `createpolicy.range.fabric` instruction.\nL104: \nL105:   * Adds support for `.level::cache_hint` qualifier for `fabric.try_get`, `fabric.try_put`, and `fabric.try_red` instructions.\nL106: ## 1.4. 【5†Document Structure】【5†】\nL107: \nL108: The information in this document is organized into the following Chapters:\nL109: \nL110:   * 【6†Programming Model】 outlines the programming model.\nL111: \nL112:   * 【7†PTX Machine Model】 gives an overview of the PTX virtual machine model.\nL113: \nL114:   * 【8†Syntax】 describes the basic syntax of the PTX language.\nL115: \nL116:   * 【9†State Spaces, Types, and Variables】 describes state spaces, types, and variable declarations.\nL117: \nL118:   * 【10†Instruction Operands】 describes instruction operands.\nL119:   * 【11†Abstracting the ABI】 describes the function and call syntax, calling convention, and PTX support for abstracting the Application Binary Interface (ABI).\nL120: \nL121:   * 【12†Instruction Set】 describes the instruction set.\nL122: \nL123:   * 【13†Special Registers】 lists special registers.\nL124: \nL125:   * 【14†Directives】 lists the assembly directives supported in PTX.\nL126: \nL127:   * 【15†Release Notes】 provides release notes for PTX ISA versions 2.x and beyond.\nL128: \nL129: References\nL130:   * 754-2008 IEEE Standard for Floating-Point Arithmetic. ISBN 978-0-7381-5752-8, 2008.\nL131: \nL132: 【16†http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/servlet/opac?punumber=4610933†ieeexplore.ieee.org】\nL133: \nL134:   * The OpenCL Specification, Version: 1.1, Document Revision: 44, June 1, 2011.\nL135: \nL136: 【17†http://www.khronos.org/registry/cl/specs/opencl-1.1.pdf†www.khronos.org】\nL137: \nL138:   * CUDA Programming Guide.\nL139: \nL140: 【18†https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/index.html】\nL141: \nL142:   * CUDA Dynamic Parallelism Programming Guide.\nL143: 【19†https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-programming-guide/04-special-topics/dynamic-parallelism.html】\nL144: \nL145:   * CUDA Atomicity Requirements.\nL146: \nL147: 【20†https://nvidia.github.io/cccl/libcudacxx/extended_api/memory_model.html#atomicity†nvidia.github.io】\nL148: \nL149:   * PTX Writers Guide to Interoperability.\nL150: \nL151: 【21†https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/ptx-writers-guide-to-interoperability/index.html】\nL152: # 2. 【6†Programming Model】【6†】\nL153: ## 2.1. 【22†A Highly Multithreaded Coprocessor】【22†】\nL154: \nL155: The GPU is a compute device capable of executing a very large number of threads in parallel. It operates as a coprocessor to the main CPU, or host: In other words, data-parallel, compute-intensive portions of applications running on the host are off-loaded onto the device.\nL156: More precisely, a portion of an application that is executed many times, but independently on different data, can be isolated into a kernel function that is executed on the GPU as many different threads. To that effect, such a function is compiled to the PTX instruction set and the resulting kernel is translated at install time to the target GPU instruction set.\nL157: ## 2.2. 【23†Thread Hierarchy】【23†】\nL158: \nL159: The batch of threads that executes a kernel is organized as a grid. A grid consists of either cooperative thread arrays or clusters of cooperative thread arrays as described in this section and illustrated in 【24†Figure 1】 and 【25†Figure 2】. Cooperative thread arrays (CTAs) implement CUDA thread blocks and clusters implement CUDA thread block clusters.\nL160: ### 2.2.1. 【26†Cooperative Thread Arrays】【26†】\nL161: \nL162: The Parallel Thread Execution (PTX) programming model is explicitly parallel: a PTX program specifies the execution of a given thread of a parallel thread array. A cooperative thread array, or CTA, is an array of threads that execute a kernel concurrently or in parallel.\nL163: Threads within a CTA can communicate with each other. To coordinate the communication of the threads within the CTA, one can specify synchronization points where threads wait until all threads in the CTA have arrived.\nL164: Each thread has a unique thread identifier within the CTA. Programs use a data parallel decomposition to partition inputs, work, and results across the threads of the CTA. Each CTA thread uses its thread identifier to determine its assigned role, assign specific input and output positions, compute addresses, and select work to perform. The thread identifier is a three-element vector `tid`, (with elements `tid.x`, `tid.y`, and `tid.z`) that specifies the thread’s position within a 1D, 2D, or 3D CTA.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[Tile] Disable execution checks in some tuple / pair helpers by miscco · Pull Request #10712 · NVIDIA/cccl · GitHub (https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/pull/10712)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/NVIDIA/cccl/pull/10712\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 416\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】Appearance settings\nL4: \nL5:   * Platform\nL6: \nL7:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL8:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL9:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL10:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL11: \nL12:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL13:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL14:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL15:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL16:       * 【8†Code ReviewManage code changes】\n